    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A trauma patient has severe facial smashing and a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) of 15. They are maintaining their own airway currently, but massive swelling is expected. What is the safest approach to intubation?', '["Rapid Sequence Intubation with Propofol and Succinylcholine", "Blind nasotracheal intubation", "Awake fiberoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy", "Observation without intubation"]', 2, 'RSI removes the patient''s spontaneous respiratory drive and tone. In severe facial trauma ("cannot ventilate, cannot intubate" risk), taking away their drive to breathe can be fatal. An awake approach is required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a tension pneumothorax also has severe hemorrhagic shock from a pelvic fracture. What would you expect their Central Venous Pressure (CVP) to be if measured via a central line?', '["Extremely low (e.g., 1 mmHg)", "Normal (e.g., 8 mmHg)", "Significantly elevated (e.g., 25 mmHg)", "Negative (e.g., -5 mmHg)"]', 2, 'Despite profound hypovolemia, the massive intrathoracic pressure from the tension pneumothorax compresses the right heart and SVC, backing up blood and causing an artificially high CVP and jugular venous distention.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient arrives in hemorrhagic shock and requires immediate vasopressors. Their INR is 5.2 due to warfarin use. Which central venous access site is the safest choice to minimize bleeding complications?', '["Subclavian vein", "Right Internal Jugular vein or Femoral vein with ultrasound guidance", "Left Subclavian vein", "External Jugular vein"]', 1, 'In coagulopathic patients, compressible sites (IJ or Femoral) are mandatory so direct pressure can be applied if bleeding occurs. The subclavian cannot be compressed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A rare neurological complication of an apically directed chest tube (placed too high into the pleural dome) is compression of the sympathetic chain. What syndrome does this cause?', '["Cauda Equina Syndrome", "Horner''s Syndrome (Ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)", "Guillain-Barre Syndrome", "SVC Syndrome"]', 1, 'The sympathetic chain runs along the apical posterior chest wall. A stiff chest tube pressing on the stellate ganglion can cause an ipsilateral Horner''s syndrome.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During the placement of a right internal jugular central line, the operator accidentally punctures a nerve running within the carotid sheath. The patient subsequently develops hoarseness. Which nerve was injured?', '["Phrenic nerve", "Vagus nerve", "Sympathetic trunk", "Spinal accessory nerve"]', 1, 'The Vagus nerve (CN X) runs in the posterior groove between the internal jugular vein and the carotid artery within the carotid sheath. Injury can paralyze the recurrent laryngeal nerve, causing hoarseness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A physician places a radial arterial line. What is the most significant, limb-threatening complication that must be monitored for?', '["Venous thrombosis", "Distal ischemia of the hand/fingers due to arterial occlusion or spasm", "Compartment syndrome of the forearm", "Carpal tunnel syndrome"]', 1, 'Arterial lines can thrombose or cause severe vasospasm, cutting off blood supply to the digits. This is why the Allen test (assessing ulnar collateral flow) is traditionally performed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An Intraosseous (IO) line is placed in a cardiac arrest patient. A nurse asks if laboratory blood tests can be drawn from the IO marrow. What is the correct response?', '["No, IO marrow cannot be used for any lab tests", "Yes, it is accurate for all standard labs including CBC and BMP", "Yes, it can be used for blood typing/crossmatch and chemistry, but WBC counts and potassium may be artifactually elevated", "Only blood cultures can be drawn from an IO"]', 2, 'Marrow blood correlates well with central venous blood for many tests (type and screen, BUN, creatinine), but the traumatic insertion often causes hemolysis (raising potassium) and marrow contains many leukocytes (falsely high WBC).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In a massively hemorrhaging patient, fluids are being pushed through an Intraosseous (IO) line. To achieve a flow rate comparable to a large peripheral IV, the IO infusion MUST be administered:', '["By gravity drip", "Using a pressure bag inflated to 300 mmHg", "Via an infusion pump set to 50 mL/hr", "Mixed with heparin"]', 1, 'The medullary cavity has high resistance. Gravity alone will only deliver a slow drip. A pressure bag or manual syringe push is required for rapid resuscitation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following fluids/medications is CONTRAINDICATED for administration through an Intraosseous (IO) line?', '["Epinephrine", "Packed Red Blood Cells", "Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN) for long-term use", "Hypertonic saline"]', 2, 'In an emergency/code setting, absolutely ANY medication or fluid (including blood) can be given via IO. However, TPN is for long-term use, and an IO must be removed within 24 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A trauma patient has a chest tube placed for a hemothorax. After the initial rush of blood, the tube continues to drain 300 mL of bright red blood every hour for the next 3 hours. What is the indicated treatment?', '["Administer tPA into the chest tube", "Clamp the chest tube", "Immediate surgical thoracotomy in the OR", "Observe for another 12 hours"]', 2, 'Continuous bleeding of >200 mL/hr for 2 to 4 hours is the classic indication that a major intrathoracic vessel is bleeding and requires surgical repair.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When removing a chest tube, the patient is asked to perform a Valsalva maneuver (take a deep breath and bear down). What is the physiological goal of this maneuver?', '["To make the patient dizzy", "To increase pleural pressure above atmospheric pressure, preventing air from rushing into the pleural space through the skin tract", "To squeeze fluid out of the lung", "To prevent a vagal response"]', 1, 'By increasing intrathoracic pressure, air is pushed OUT of the tract as the tube is pulled, preventing an iatrogenic pneumothorax.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is intubated in the ED. The physician suspects the tube has been pushed too deep into the right mainstem bronchus. What is the simplest, immediate maneuver to correct and confirm this before getting a chest X-ray?', '["Extubate and re-intubate", "Deflate the cuff, pull the tube back 2-3 cm, re-inflate the cuff, and listen for the return of left-sided breath sounds", "Push the tube further", "Perform a needle decompression"]', 1, 'Pulling the tube back slightly almost always withdraws it from the right mainstem back into the trachea, instantly restoring ventilation to the left lung.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an uncomplicated, central line-associated bloodstream infection (CRBSI) caused by coagulase-negative Staphylococci (e.g., S. epidermidis) requires the line to be removed. What is the typical duration of systemic antibiotic therapy required after removal?', '["1-2 days", "5-7 days", "10-14 days", "4-6 weeks"]', 2, 'Uncomplicated CRBSIs (fever resolves within 72 hours, no endocarditis or osteomyelitis) are typically treated for 10-14 days after the infected catheter is removed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient''s central line blood culture returns positive for Candida albicans (yeast). The ICU team wants to "salvage" the line by giving antifungal medications through it. What is the standard of care?', '["Treat with Fluconazole and leave the line in place", "Catheter salvage is absolutely contraindicated in fungemia; the line MUST be removed immediately", "Exchange the line over a guidewire", "Administer Amphotericin B locks"]', 1, 'Fungal CRBSIs (Candida) and Staphylococcus aureus CRBSIs are highly virulent, form dense biofilms, and carry a massive risk of endocarditis or blindness (endophthalmitis). The line MUST be pulled.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient in cardiac arrest has electrical activity on the monitor (e.g., sinus tachycardia) but no palpable pulse. This is Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA). Bedside ultrasound shows the heart is actually beating vigorously. What does this "Pseudo-PEA" indicate?', '["The patient is dead and resuscitation should stop", "There is an extreme low-flow state (obstructive or severe hypovolemic shock) preventing a palpable pulse, and the underlying cause must be fixed instantly (e.g., tension pneumothorax, tamponade)", "The patient has ventricular fibrillation", "The ultrasound is broken"]', 1, 'Pseudo-PEA means the heart is trying to pump, but either there is no volume to pump (hemorrhage) or an obstruction is blocking flow (tamponade/PE/tension pneumo). Relieving the obstruction often restores a pulse instantly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient who was intubated in the ICU for 3 weeks presents to the ED a month later with progressive inspiratory stridor and shortness of breath. What iatrogenic complication is most likely?', '["Recurrent pneumonia", "Tracheal stenosis from prolonged high endotracheal tube cuff pressure causing mucosal necrosis", "Asthma", "Vocal cord polyps"]', 1, 'Prolonged intubation with cuff pressures >30 cm H2O causes ischemic necrosis of the tracheal rings, which heal with thick scar tissue, narrowing the trachea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During RSI, a patient in profound septic shock requires induction. Standard doses of induction agents (like Propofol) will likely cause fatal cardiovascular collapse. What is the recommended dosing strategy for induction agents and paralytics in severe shock?', '["Give double the dose of Propofol to ensure sleep", "Give a reduced dose (e.g., 1/2 or 1/4) of a cardio-stable induction agent (Ketamine/Etomidate), and a FULL or DOUBLE dose of paralytic (Rocuronium/Succinylcholine)", "Give no induction agent, only paralytic", "Give full dose induction, half dose paralytic"]', 1, 'In shock, the brain requires very little drug to go to sleep, but standard doses will cause vasodilation and death. Conversely, because blood flow to the muscles is terribly slow, paralytics take much longer to work, so a full or double dose is used to ensure rapid paralysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A morbidly obese patient requires intubation. Laying them flat (supine) causes immediate oxygen desaturation. How should they be positioned for preoxygenation and intubation?', '["Prone", "Left lateral decubitus", "Ramped position (head, neck, and shoulders elevated 20-30 degrees) to align the sternal notch with the external auditory meatus", "Trendelenburg"]', 2, 'Ramping uses gravity to pull the heavy chest tissue and abdominal contents down and away from the diaphragm, massively improving functional residual capacity and the laryngoscopy view.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following accurately describes the difference between the Sellick maneuver and the BURP maneuver during intubation?', '["They are the same thing", "Sellick applies pressure to the thyroid cartilage to improve the view; BURP applies pressure to the cricoid to prevent aspiration", "Sellick applies pressure to the cricoid cartilage to occlude the esophagus; BURP applies pressure to the thyroid cartilage to move the vocal cords into view", "Sellick is for pediatrics; BURP is for adults"]', 2, 'Sellick = Cricoid pressure for aspiration (controversial). BURP = Backward, Upward, Rightward, Posterior pressure on the thyroid cartilage to improve the glottic view.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient arrives with a stab wound to the left chest. They are profoundly hypotensive, have distended neck veins, and the trachea is deviated to the RIGHT. Breath sounds are absent on the LEFT. What is the diagnosis?', '["Right-sided tension pneumothorax", "Left-sided tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Massive left hemothorax"]', 1, 'Air filling the left pleural space under tension pushes the mediastinum and trachea away from the injury (to the right), compressing the vena cava (JVD) and causing shock (hypotension).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents with sudden, sharp, right-sided chest pain and shortness of breath. Their blood pressure is normal (120/80), heart rate is 90, and trachea is midline. Breath sounds are decreased on the right. What is the diagnosis?', '["Tension pneumothorax", "Simple spontaneous pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Myocardial infarction"]', 1, 'A simple pneumothorax causes pain and dyspnea but does NOT cause hemodynamic compromise (hypotension/shock) or tracheal deviation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During the insertion of a central line, you inadvertently pull the guidewire back while the needle is still in the patient''s neck. Why is it an absolute rule to NEVER re-advance a guidewire through the needle once it has been withdrawn?', '["It increases the risk of infection", "The sharp bevel of the needle can shear off the tip of the guidewire, causing a wire embolus into the heart", "It will cause the wire to knot", "It dulls the needle"]', 1, 'If the wire must be withdrawn, the needle and wire must be removed together as a single unit. Re-advancing the wire risks slicing it on the sharp needle edge.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient requires a chest tube for a simple, 40% spontaneous pneumothorax. Which of the following is true comparing a small-bore pigtail catheter (14F) to a large-bore surgical chest tube (32F) for this specific indication?', '["The large-bore tube resolves the pneumothorax faster", "The pigtail catheter is equally effective for evacuating air and causes significantly less pain", "The pigtail catheter has a higher risk of causing a tension pneumothorax", "Only the large-bore tube can be connected to a Heimlich valve"]', 1, 'Air requires very little diameter to flow. Small pigtail catheters placed via Seldinger technique are now the standard of care for simple pneumothoraces due to equal efficacy and reduced morbidity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A pleural fluid analysis reveals a pH of 7.10, Glucose of 40 mg/dL, and an LDH of 1500 U/L. What is the diagnosis and required management?', '["Transudative effusion; treat with diuretics", "Empyema (complicated parapneumonic effusion); requires immediate chest tube drainage", "Simple parapneumonic effusion; treat with antibiotics only", "Hemothorax; requires surgery"]', 1, 'An acidic pH (< 7.2) and low glucose (< 60) in pleural fluid indicate active bacterial metabolism (pus/empyema). Antibiotics alone cannot penetrate this; it must be mechanically drained.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When inserting a chest tube for a hemothorax, the tube is directed posteriorly and inferiorly. Why?', '["To avoid the heart", "Because blood and fluid accumulate in the dependent, postero-inferior portions of the chest due to gravity", "To avoid the brachial plexus", "Because it is easier to secure the tube in that direction"]', 1, 'Gravity pulls fluid down into the posterior costodiaphragmatic recesses. A tube aimed apically will miss the blood entirely.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'You are attempting to place an Intraosseous (IO) line in a severe trauma patient. Both legs have obvious, gross deformities (tibial fractures). Where is the next best site for IO placement?', '["The fractured tibia (distal to the fracture)", "Proximal humerus or the sternum", "The calcaneus", "The radius"]', 1, 'An IO cannot be placed in a fractured bone because the fluid will extravasate. The proximal humerus (or sternum, using a specialized FAST1 device) are the primary alternative sites.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In pediatric intubation, why is Atropine sometimes given as a premedication before administering Succinylcholine?', '["To increase blood pressure", "To prevent the profound vagal-mediated bradycardia that infants experience during laryngoscopy and from the succinylcholine itself", "To dry up secretions", "To paralyze the patient faster"]', 1, 'Infants are highly vagotonic. Stimulation of the airway, combined with the parasympathetic effects of succinylcholine, can cause life-threatening bradycardia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the correct formula to estimate the size of an UNCUFFED endotracheal tube for a pediatric patient older than 1 year?', '["(Age in years / 4) + 4", "(Age in years / 4) + 3.5", "(Age in months / 2) + 2", "(Weight in kg / 2) + 4"]', 0, 'The Cole formula for uncuffed tubes is (Age/4) + 4. For cuffed tubes, it is (Age/4) + 3.5. (e.g., an 8-year-old gets a 6.0 uncuffed or 5.5 cuffed).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which high-pressure ventilation technique is used exclusively with a needle cricothyrotomy to provide temporary oxygenation in a "cannot intubate, cannot ventilate" pediatric emergency?', '["BiPAP", "Translaryngeal Jet Ventilation", "High-frequency oscillatory ventilation", "Standard Bag-Valve-Mask ventilation through the needle"]', 1, 'A BVM cannot generate enough pressure to force air through a tiny 14-gauge catheter. Jet ventilation using a 50 psi wall oxygen source is required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During translaryngeal jet ventilation, the operator must ensure a prolonged expiratory phase (e.g., 1 second of inflation, 4 seconds of exhalation). Why?', '["To prevent hyperventilation", "To allow passive exhalation of air through the patient''s native upper airway, preventing massive barotrauma and tension pneumothorax", "To save oxygen", "To stimulate spontaneous breathing"]', 1, 'Jet ventilation actively forces air in, but exhalation is completely passive. If the airway is totally obstructed, or if exhalation time is too short, air rapidly accumulates and pops the lungs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A surgical cricothyrotomy involves incising the skin and then the cricothyroid membrane. What is the standard incision pattern to maximize safety?', '["Horizontal skin, vertical membrane", "Vertical skin, vertical membrane", "Vertical skin incision, followed by a horizontal stab incision through the membrane", "Horizontal skin, horizontal membrane"]', 2, 'A vertical skin incision avoids the lateral anterior jugular veins and allows the incision to be extended up or down if landmarks are poor. The membrane itself is incised horizontally to fit the tube.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which central venous access site has the absolute lowest risk of iatrogenic pneumothorax?', '["Right Internal Jugular", "Left Internal Jugular", "Subclavian", "Femoral"]', 3, 'The femoral vein is located in the groin, far away from the thoracic cavity, making pneumothorax biologically impossible.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When comparing the Internal Jugular (IJ) and Subclavian approaches for central line placement, which of the following is true?', '["The Subclavian site is generally more comfortable for the patient and has lower infection rates, but the IJ is compressible and easier to guide with ultrasound", "The IJ site has lower infection rates than the Subclavian", "The Subclavian site is compressible", "Ultrasound is useless for the IJ approach"]', 0, 'Subclavian lines don''t move with neck rotation (comfortable) and stay clean. IJ lines are dirty (drool/sweat) and uncomfortable, but they are safe to place because you can compress bleeding and easily use ultrasound.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'You are evaluating vessels in the neck with an ultrasound probe prior to central line placement. You apply gentle downward pressure. The vessel you are looking at completely collapses and disappears. This vessel is:', '["The internal jugular vein", "The carotid artery", "A nerve bundle", "A lymph node"]', 0, 'Veins are thin-walled, low-pressure capacitance vessels that easily compress under probe pressure. Arteries are thick, muscular, and pulsatile, resisting compression.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During the Seldinger technique, what is the most catastrophic error an operator can make regarding the guidewire?', '["Inserting the wire too fast", "Using a wire that is too long", "Letting go of the wire completely, allowing it to be lost into the patient''s vascular system", "Coating the wire in saline"]', 2, 'A "retained guidewire" is a severe, never-event that requires emergency endovascular retrieval by interventional radiology to prevent arrhythmias or thrombosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A physician realizes they have accidentally placed a large 7-French central venous catheter directly into the Subclavian artery. What is the strict, immediate protocol?', '["Pull the catheter out and hold pressure for 20 minutes", "Leave the catheter exactly where it is, secure it, and consult Vascular Surgery immediately for surgical or endovascular removal", "Inject protamine", "Push the catheter all the way in"]', 1, 'The subclavian artery cannot be compressed. Pulling a large hole-punch (the 7F catheter) out of it will cause the patient to rapidly bleed to death into their chest or neck.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When removing a chest tube, a purse-string suture is immediately tied tight as the tube is pulled out. This is done to:', '["Stop bleeding from the intercostal artery", "Instantly seal the tract to prevent air from being sucked into the chest, causing a new pneumothorax", "Make the scar look better", "Prevent infection"]', 1, 'The chest cavity has negative pressure. An open hole will immediately suck in room air. The purse-string suture tightly closes the tissue defect instantly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on a ventilator is being assessed for extubation. They are awake, following commands, and can lift their head off the pillow for 5 seconds. Why is the 5-second head lift an important criterion?', '["It proves they are not brain dead", "It indicates adequate recovery of neuromuscular strength, suggesting they can clear secretions and protect their airway from aspiration", "It stretches the neck for extubation", "It proves the paralytic was not given"]', 1, 'Head lift and a strong cough are clinical markers of core muscle strength, ensuring the patient won''t immediately fail and need re-intubation due to airway collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an anticipated difficult airway (e.g., massive goiter) is to be intubated. You decide to use a Gum Elastic Bougie. Which laryngoscopic view (Cormack-Lehane) is the Bougie specifically designed to overcome?', '["Grade 1 (Full view)", "Grade 2 (Partial view)", "Grade 3 (Only the epiglottis is visible)", "Grade 4 (Only the soft palate is visible)"]', 2, 'In a Grade 3 view, the operator can see the epiglottis but not the cords. The bougie can be blindly slipped underneath the epiglottis. The angled tip will bounce off the tracheal rings ("clicks"), confirming placement before the tube is passed over it.'),
