    -- SECTION 6: MIXED CASE STUDIES, REMOVAL CRITERIA, AND COMPLICATIONS (251-300)
    -- ADDITIONAL 11 QUESTIONS TO REACH EXACTLY 300
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common cause of Intraosseous (IO) needle failure during an emergency resuscitation?', '["Needle blockage by bone marrow", "Insertion too deep, causing the needle to pass completely through the posterior cortex of the bone", "Using a needle that is too thick", "The patient''s bone is too hard"]', 1, 'Pushing the needle too forcefully often results in "through-and-through" puncture, where the tip exits the back of the bone, leading to fluid extravasation into the soft tissues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'To comply with maximal sterile barrier precautions for central line insertion, which of the following must the operator wear?', '["Only sterile gloves", "Sterile gloves and a mask", "A cap, mask, sterile gown, and sterile gloves", "A cap and mask only"]', 2, 'Maximal barriers (cap, mask, sterile gown, sterile gloves, and a large full-body drape) are the standard of care to prevent catheter-related bloodstream infections.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When using a Macintosh (curved) laryngoscope blade, where is the tip correctly placed to visualize the glottis?', '["Directly under the epiglottis", "In the vallecula, to indirectly lift the epiglottis via the hyoepiglottic ligament", "Against the vocal cords", "In the esophagus"]', 1, 'The Macintosh blade is designed to sit in the vallecula. Lifting forward puts tension on the hyoepiglottic ligament, which pulls the epiglottis up out of the way.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Succinylcholine is strictly contraindicated in which of the following patients due to the risk of life-threatening hyperkalemia?', '["A patient with a simple bone fracture", "A patient 5 days post-massive burn or crush injury", "A patient with acute appendicitis", "A patient with a normal potassium of 4.0"]', 1, 'After 48-72 hours of a major burn, crush, or denervation injury, acetylcholine receptors upregulate across the entire muscle membrane. Succinylcholine causes massive potassium release in these patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the typical onset time of Rocuronium for facilitating tracheal intubation?', '["10 seconds", "60 to 90 seconds", "5 minutes", "10 minutes"]', 1, 'Rocuronium is a fast-acting nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker, typically providing excellent intubating conditions within 60-90 seconds.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In a standard 3-chamber chest drainage system (Pleur-evac), what is the typical recommended initial suction level?', '["-5 cm H2O", "-20 cm H2O", "-50 cm H2O", "0 cm H2O (Water seal only)"]', 1, 'A suction level of -20 cm H2O is the physiological standard for most adult chest tubes to facilitate the evacuation of air and fluid.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When evaluating a difficult airway using the MOANS mnemonic, what does the "N" stand for?', '["Nose obstruction", "No teeth (edentulous)", "Neck mobility", "Narrow glottis"]', 1, 'MOANS: Mask seal, Obesity/Obstruction, Age (>55), No teeth (makes mask sealing difficult), Stiff (resistance to ventilation).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a central line procedure, if you accidentally puncture the carotid artery with a small finder needle, what is the most appropriate management?', '["Immediate surgery", "Withdraw the needle and apply firm, direct pressure for at least 10 minutes", "Insert the large catheter anyway", "Nothing, it will heal on its own"]', 1, 'A small needle puncture (e.g., 22G or 20G) is usually benign if compressed immediately and firmly for a sufficient amount of time.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is having their chest tube removed. At what phase of respiration should the tube be rapidly pulled to prevent air entry into the pleural space?', '["During a forceful cough", "At end-expiration or at peak inspiration (Valsalva maneuver)", "During rapid shallow breathing", "During a deep yawn"]', 1, 'Pulling the tube while the patient holds their breath (Valsalva) ensures positive intrathoracic pressure, pushing air out of the tract rather than sucking it in.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "gold standard" for the objective confirmation of proper endotracheal tube placement in the trachea?', '["Listening for breath sounds", "Visualization of ''mist'' in the tube", "Continuous waveform capnography showing a consistent CO2 trace", "Chest X-ray"]', 2, 'Waveform capnography is the most reliable, real-time method to confirm that the tube is in the trachea and that the patient is being ventilated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When performing the Seldinger technique for a central line, what is the correct action if the guidewire meets resistance while being advanced through the needle?', '["Force it through", "Stop immediately, withdraw the wire and needle as a unit, and re-attempt", "Twist the wire forcefully", "Use a hammer"]', 1, 'Forcing a wire against resistance can cause vessel perforation, wire kinking, or wire shearing. You must stop, pull back, and re-orient.');

